WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi,
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.230
and this is AI
Simplifed, where we

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.510
learn to make our data useful.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.280
In this series,
we've been learning

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.700
about ingesting data,
training models, and getting

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:12.600
predictions.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.050
It would be handy to learn how
to automate all of these tasks

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.420
by implementing them
programmatically.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.000
In this video, we will
learn how to do exactly

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.385
that with the Vertex AI SDK.

00:00:23.385 --> 00:00:25.470
Now, before we
dive into the SDK,

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.300
let's take a quick
look at the options

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.400
to interact with Vertex AI.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.350
There is Google Cloud Console.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.860
It is the UI to work with
our model assets, the data

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.160
sets, the endpoints, and
the predictions in Cloud.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.340
Now, so far we've been using
the console in this series,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.480
so you must be familiar.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.240
But if not, I've included the
links to the previous episode

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.020
below.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.930
Now, anything that you
can do through the UI,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.150
you can also do
programmatically.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:56.070
And for that, we have the
Google Cloud Vertex AI SDK.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.830
And then there are Vertex
AI client libraries,

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:03.000
which help us make calls
to the Vertex AI API.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.220
Now, the benefits of
using client library

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.740
is that they are
optimized for languages

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.360
that you choose to use--

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.750
for example, Java,
Python, or Node.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.600
Now the question becomes
when to use the console

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.520
and when to use the SDK.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.970
The UI helps us get familiar
with the features and functions

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:24.210
available in Vertex AI.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:29.730
So for that reason, using the
UI is my go-to recommendation

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.160
if you are new to
machine learning and AI,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.680
or just prefer to
do things visually.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.900
The SDK is best
if you're looking

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.190
to programmatically implement
the steps in your ML workflow.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.160
Now, if you are an experienced
machine learning engineer

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.720
or data scientist,
then you would

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.930
benefit from the ease of
automating your workflow.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.370
If SDK isn't available in
the language of your choice,

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:59.300
then you can use the client
libraries or the REST API.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.240
Now that we know the different
ways to interact with Vertex

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.300
AI, we need a fun example
to play with and create

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.250
a model using the SDK.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.160
I've picked out the
perfect data set for this--

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.000
beans.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.670
Well, who does not like beans?

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:17.060
Being a math nerd, I found
out that Pythagoras--

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.720
yeah, the one who came up
with a squared plus b squared

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.420
equals c squared,
that Pythagoras--

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.880
had an aversion to beans.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.180
Who would have thought, right?

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.420
OK, so here's the game plan.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:33.380
Overall, we will get the
data, explore it a little bit,

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.960
then create a
model and deploy it

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:39.450
to an endpoint from which
we can make predictions.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.760
And we will do all of
this using the SDK.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.890
We will need the tools
to explore the data

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.050
and prototype this ML model.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.240
Now for this, we
will use notebooks

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.660
within Vertex, which is the
hosted JupyterLab Notebooks.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.960
They come preinstalled with
your favorite machine learning

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.215
workflows, such as
TensorFlow or PyTorch.

00:03:01.215 --> 00:03:02.840
These frameworks are
already installed,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.150
and you also have
customized hardware,

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.540
so you can use the CPUs and
the GPUs if you need it.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:12.665
It also integrates with Git, so
we can customize and initialize

00:03:12.665 --> 00:03:15.860
a Git repo within the
notebook, and it also

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.770
integrates with other
Google Cloud services.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.020
So we'll put our
data into BigQuery,

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.840
which is Google Cloud
enterprise data warehouse,

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.230
and inside the notebook,
we will download that data

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:31.040
from BigQuery and
then put it into use

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.190
to explore that data
using pandas DataFrame.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:38.390
And then the next step would
be to create our training

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:41.070
job, creating the
Vertex AI model,

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.430
deploying it, and then
making predictions,

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.490
and then finally monitoring
that job in the console.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.140
Now, ready to go?

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.810
Let's see it.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:54.380
Now in the Vertex AI console,
we can create a hosted Jupyter

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.160
Notebook.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.450
And if we click
on a New Instance,

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.840
we see that there are
different types of notebooks

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.410
that we can choose from.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:04.400
They come preinstalled with
our favorite machine learning

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.180
frameworks, and we can add
GPUs if needed, and can also

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.160
install additional libraries
after the notebook is created.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.800
I've already created a notebook
here, so let's get into it.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.550
The first step is to
download our data.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:23.320
I'm using Dry Beans Dataset from
UCI, which I have linked below.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.480
This is a tabular
data set that was

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.690
taken from images for
different types of beans,

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:32.650
and different data points were
collected on each type of bean.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.230
In manufacturing and
other industries,

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:39.040
it's common to extract
tabular metadata from images

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.530
and use that to train a model.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.500
I've already uploaded
the data in BigQuery,

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:48.190
and we can see the different
data points in the schema.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.430
The label that we are
predicting is called Class,

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.470
and we can run this
[? quick ?] query

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:57.160
in the data set to see roughly
what the distribution of bean

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.960
type is across our data set.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.340
So here, I'm using the
BigQuery client library

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:08.290
to download that data inside my
notebook as pandas DataFrame.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:10.420
And using this
pandas method, we can

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:15.320
see a plot of how the data
is distributed by parameters.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.570
Next, we use the Seaborn library
to create a pair plot, which

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:22.180
can help us identify
our relationships

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.850
between the features in
our data and the label

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.140
column within the class.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:30.610
Now that we have a good idea
of what our data looks like

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.610
and how it is balanced,
we are ready to kick off

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.240
the training job in Vertex AI.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.490
We initialize the project
that we want to use.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.860
Then to train this
model using AutoML,

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.870
we first need a data set.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.350
We can pass the URL to
the data in BigQuery

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:49.060
directly to Vertex AI.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.820
There's no need to
manually move our data--

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.160
Vertex will handle that for us.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.980
Now if we go in Vertex AI
console into our data set,

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.400
we can see our
created data set here

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.360
and then we can
see more details,

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.190
such as the schema and the
data source in BigQuery.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.020
We could kick off the
training job here,

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.120
but I trust that you
can click a button,

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:13.210
so let's stick to
the SDK for this one.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.910
Back in our notebook,
here is the code

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:17.740
to create the training job.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:21.490
This is a classification
model, where we are classifying

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:23.500
the beans into seven classes.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:26.980
So we define that and pass
all the different features

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:29.830
that we want to use in
training this model.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.800
You could add more
options here, such as how

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.840
you would want
AutoML to optimize

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:38.390
the training, specifically
useful for an imbalanced data

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:38.890
set.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:40.840
I'm not doing that here.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.560
Finally, job.run will
run the training job.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.710
We specify the data set
we want to use, the target

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.220
column or label that we are
predicting, how long we want

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.610
to run the training for,
and enable early stopping,

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.820
so Vertex AI stops the
training if it does not

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.520
see that the model can
be further improved.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:05.200
Now you specify this, which is
useful to keep the cost low.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.180
Once the model has trained--

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:08.680
this could take some time--

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:13.000
we can see in the console that
the training job has finished.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.700
And we can see the
evaluation metrics

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.460
to see how our model
actually performed.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:22.060
The confusion matrix shows
how good a job our model

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:25.060
did in distinguishing
the different bean types.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.580
Since this is a tabular
model, Vertex AI

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:31.930
also gives us feature importance
out of the box with AutoML.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.270
We can see the features
in our data set

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.540
that signaled our model's
prediction the most.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.630
Here, it looks like shape
and eccentricity were

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:43.000
the most important features.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.980
Now that we have
our model created,

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:47.470
let's deploy our
endpoints so we can

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:49.810
use it to serve predictions.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.120
You can do that
right here in the UI,

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.510
but let me show you how
to do that in the SDK.

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:57.940
All you have to do
to deploy the model

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:03.130
is model.deploy and the type of
machine type you want to use.

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.480
There are more things
you can specify here,

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.840
but I'm just going
with a minimum.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.720
Once created, we can
see it in the console

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.050
and test to make predictions
from our endpoint.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.390
Let's head back over
into our notebook

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:20.110
and see how to make
predictions using the API.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:22.090
We provide a test set.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:25.240
Then we need to create
the endpoint object,

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.470
grab the resource name from the
deployed model and use it here,

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:32.169
and then we use that
to make a prediction

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:34.720
request to our endpoint.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.840
And there's our prediction.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.470
We just trained an AutoML
model and made a prediction

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:43.150
using the SDK.

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:47.110
We could use the same data set
to train a custom model, too,

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.940
all using the SDK.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.980
Here are the code samples
that can help you do that.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:53.110
Try it out yourself.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:56.060
And if you need help,
refer to the links below.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:59.800
All right, so today we created
a bean classification model

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:03.100
using the Vertex AI Python SDK.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.780
Give this a try
yourself, and let

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:07.890
me know how it goes
in the comments below.

